#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""standby-agent - the Claude Code engine behind Standby, wherever Claude Code runs.

A question comes in from the Standby app, and this invokes headless Claude Code
with Standby's own rules, the user's own skills, and read access to the live
transcript. The answer goes back out.

Design notes:

* The engine is real Claude Code (`claude -p`), so it has the same skills,
  CLAUDE.md and judgement as an interactive session.
* It is held open. Measured: a cold `claude -p` costs about
  9 s of process startup before it even reaches the model, while a warm turn in
  a persistent process came back in 2.2 s. So `serve` keeps one process per
  meeting, talking newline-delimited JSON over stdin/stdout, and pays startup
  once when the meeting starts.
* Continuity comes from a per-meeting session UUID, so the brief and earlier
  answers stay in context, prompt caching keeps later answers cheap, and a
  restart can resume the same conversation.
* Latency is not reliable. In the same measured run, two turns came back in
  2.2 s and 7.7 s while two others stalled 60 s and 70 s, nearly all of it
  before the request was even sent (throttling, not generation). So every turn
  has a deadline, and blowing the deadline is a normal outcome with a useful
  answer: park the question and say so.
* The meeting agent is the user's own Claude Code: their MCP servers, plugins
  and skills, in auto mode (or only the pre-approved tools where a build has no
  auto mode). The voice runs without MCP servers, on the transcript and the
  meeting agent's notes only, so it answers fast.
* The child's stderr goes to a file, never to a pipe. An unread stderr pipe
  will deadlock a long-lived child once its buffer fills.

Python 3.6+.
"""

from __future__ import print_function

import argparse
import base64
import glob
import datetime
import json
import os
import random
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
